{"pagination":{"maxPageableSet":"10000","numFound":1,"query":"name=Feynman&limit=10&title=Six Easy Pieces","limit":10,"start":0},"items":{"mods":{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":{"title":"Six not-so-easy pieces ","subTitle":"Einstein's relativity, symmetry, and space-time "},"name":[{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":"Feynman, Richard Phillips.","role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}}},{"@type":"personal","namePart":"Penrose, Roger","role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"writer of introduction"}}}],"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"bibliography"},{"@authority":"aat","#text":"sound recordings."},{"@authority":"lcgft","#text":"Sound recordings"},{"@authority":"rvmgf","#text":"Enregistrements sonores."}],"originInfo":{"place":[{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"mau"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"Massachusetts"}]},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"Reading, Mass"}}],"dateIssued":[{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"1997"},"c1997"],"issuance":"monographic","agent":{"namePart":"Addison-Wesley Pub."}},"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":[{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"xxvii, 152 p. : ill. ; 24 cm."},{"form":{"@type":"Illustrative content","#text":"illustration."}}],"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"No single breakthrough in twentieth-century physics (with the possible exception of quantum mechanics) changed our view of the world more than that of Einstein's discovery of relativity. The notions that the flow of time is not a constant, that the mass of an object depends on its velocity, and that the speed of light is a constant no matter what the motion of the observer, at first seemed shocking to scientists and laymen alike. But, as Feynman shows so clearly and so entertainingly in the lectures chosen for this volume, these crazy notions are no mere dry principles of physics, but are things of beauty and elegance. No one - not even Einstein himself - explained these difficult, anti-intuitive concepts more clearly, or with more verve and gusto, than Richard Feynman."},"tableOfContents":{"@type":"Contents","#text":"Vectors -- Symmetry in physical laws -- The special theory of relativity -- Relativistic energy and momentum -- Space-time -- Curved space."},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Richard P. Feynman ; originally prepared for publication by Robert B. Leighton and Matthew Sands ; new introduction by Roger Penrose."},{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references and index."}],"subject":[{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Symmetry (Physics)"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Special relativity (Physics)"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Space and time"},{"@authority":"lcsh","geographic":"Space and time"}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"QC793.3.S9 F49 1997"},{"@edition":"21","@authority":"ddc","#text":"530.11"}],"relatedItem":[{"titleInfo":{"title":"Helix books"}},{"@type":"otherFormat","@otherType":"Online version:","@displayLabel":"Online version:","titleInfo":{"title":"Six not-so-easy pieces"},"originInfo":{"publisher":"Reading, Mass. : Addison-Wesley Pub., ©1997"},"identifier":{"@type":"local","#text":"(OCoLC)623015336"},"name":{"namePart":"Feynman, Richard Phillips."}},{"@type":"series","titleInfo":{"title":"Helix book"}},{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/990076051680203941/catalog"}}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"0201150255"},{"@type":"lccn","#text":"^^^96047811^"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"957495729"}],"location":[{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","#text":"Harvard Plate Stacks, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"QC793.3.S9 F49 1997"},null,null],"extension":[{"librarycloud":{"HarvardRepositories":{"Harva